{
  "id": "P1-W02-M4",
  "title": "A commit is a snapshot. A branch is a sticky note.",
  "alt": "On the left, Git's object model: a commit box pointing to a tree pointing to a blob, with the blob's SHA-1 computed by hand from the header and content and matching git rev-parse. On the right, the same starting graph resolved two ways \u2014 merge produces a commit M with two parents and rewrites nothing, while rebase replays D and E as new commits D-prime and E-prime and leaves the originals crossed out in red but recoverable through the reflog. A shrinking bar ladder shows git bisect closing 200 commits in eight test runs.",
  "width": 1000,
  "height": 660,
  "tier": "T3",
  "week": 2,
